From fd5230959d4233c489c4f82d0a03ce04425e4066 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:10:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: a stream that the DRM PRIME decoder cannot take
 goes to the FFmpeg decoder

Where no decoder of the build decodes to DRM PRIME in hardware (the
Generic x86 image), CDVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME decodes in software into
8-bit pictures only. For a stream of more bits its get_format() finds no
format, FFmpeg refuses every packet (EPERM), AddData() says "send it
again", and VideoPlayer sent the same packet for ever, with no picture.
Open() declined such a stream from its hints, but the hints can be
wrong: a transport stream that is joined live is opened without
avformat_find_stream_info(), and a 10-bit HEVC channel then has a hint
of 8 bits and no profile (the hint that the VAAPI bit-depth fix no
longer trusts). With "Allow using DRM PRIME decoder" on, such a channel
never showed a picture.

The format that the decoder offers now decides, whatever the hints said
(CDRMPRIMEHandBack):
- get_format() refuses: AddData() leaves every packet with VideoPlayer,
  and GetPicture() answers the new VC_HANDBACK;
- VideoPlayer then opens the stream again with CODEC_HANDED_BACK, which
  the factory reads as "no platform decoder": CDVDVideoCodecFFmpeg takes
  it, with VAAPI where it is on, as with DRM PRIME off. Only a platform
  decoder hands a stream back and none is tried for one handed back, so
  it happens once per opening and cannot loop (as VC_REOPEN gives a
  failed hardware decoder's stream to the software one);
- so that the next decoder starts where this one started, VideoPlayer
  keeps the packets that the DRM PRIME decoder took until it has taken a
  format (its converge count, which was 0: up to 300, as
  CDVDVideoCodecFFmpeg keeps them since a key frame), sends them again
  ahead of the one it put back, and drops them once the decoder keeps
  none. That last line changes nothing for the other decoders:
  CDVDVideoCodecFFmpeg's count is never 0 after a packet, and the
  others' always is, with nothing kept.
Open() still declines what its hints say is of more bits, and now also
a hint of 8 bits with a profile of more (HEVC Main 10, H.264 High 10,
VP9 profile 2 or 3), which it took before.

A format refused after one was taken (a stream that changes to more
bits on the way) has no packets kept: the FFmpeg decoder gives pictures
from the next key frame on.

Host test (handback_test.cpp: FFmpeg 9.0.2's own decoders under a model
of VideoPlayer's video thread and of the decoder's AddData() and
GetPicture(), with the gate and the rule of the tree): the 10-bit HEVC
transport stream opened without stream info (hint 8 bits, no profile)
is taken by Open(), refused by get_format(), handed back once, and gives
the 96 pictures of a decoder fed from the start, the same and in order,
with 32 threads (one packet kept and sent again) and with one; the tree
before sends one packet more than 1000 times with no picture. The same
stream in Matroska is declined by Open(). An 8-bit H.264 stream stays
with the DRM PRIME decoder, with every picture and nothing kept.
engine_test checks the rule and the gate (456 checks); 11 mutations of
them make it fail, and 3 of the gate make the hand-back test fail.
